so that this would work

```fsharp
let inline show< ^A when ^A : (static member show : ^A  -> string) > (a : ^A) =
    ( ^A : (static member Show : ^A -> string) a )

type System.String with static member Show(a : System.String) = sprintf "{String: %A}" a

type System.Boolean with static member Show(a : System.Boolean) = sprintf "{Boolean: %A}" a

type MyType<'a> = | MyType of 'a 

type MyType<'a> = with
    static member Show(v: MyType<'a>) = 
        match v with
        | MyType a -> sprintf "{MyType: %A}" (show a)

[<EntryPoint>]
let main args = 
    let o = show "myself"               // {String: myself}
    let p = show true                   // {Boolean: true}
    let q = show 1                      // compiler error as no Int.Show in place
    let r = show (MyType "my way")      // {MyType: {String: my way}}
    let s = show (MyType 1)             // compiler error as no Int.Show in place
    let s = show (MyType (MyType true)) // {MyType: {MyType: {String: true}}}

    0
```

@Alxandr I agree, it would be nice if we could explictly state intention here and I agree in the absence of this the error messages at the call site will be pretty surprising especially for newbies.  
However the stuff that @TobyShaw is working on is more to extend SRTPs to allow something type-classish.
My hope would be that real TCs will be much easier (and more feature rich) than this. But since its not clear when (or for that matter IF) TCs come I am happy to work with allowing extension methods to be picked up by constrained functions

> typeclass without HKTs is of little use

Even without HKTs, personally I would love to be able to abandon interfaces in my daily F# usage. Type classes/traits/protocols as "better interfaces" is very valuable by itself.

@ShalokShalom Last time I checked, Elm did not have anything remotely similar to type classes or any form of overloading, for that matter. So I wouldn't say that following the Elm example is helpful.

@thautwarm 

> That's because F# has the SRTPs, which provide some essential capabilities.

No, I think he refers to the HKTs encoding you mention with injection and projection that can currently be done using inheritance, see https://github.com/palladin/Higher no need to SRTPs, so it incurs in some runtime overhead.

@ShalokShalom JVM is easy, cause it does type erasure on generics. Basically, `List<Foo>` gets compiled to `List<Object>`, so all generics is just a compile-time trick. This is why Scala can get away with doing stuff like `Monad[_]`, cause the JVM doesn't have to understand it, only the compiler does.

*Edit*

This is also why you can't do `List<int>` in java, cause you can only do generics with "pointers" (ie, reference types).

@alexandru Thanks for this perspective, it's wonderful. I think a key difference here is that the BCL (or CoreFX as it's called for .NET Core) is seen as well-designed enough that most F# developers use it as their base. There are some neat wrappers for various APIs that make F# consumption nicer, but my general impression is that there isn't an "F# standard library", either as a single unit or composed of multiple smaller libraries that effectively replaces CoreFX.

Judicious use of excellent libraries such as [FSharpPlus](https://github.com/fsprojects/FSharpPlus) definitely get you closer to the Typelevel kind of programming that many Scala developers enjoy. But this (and other libraries, like [Higher](https://github.com/palladin/Higher) aren't nearly as prevalent in usage. One thing we've discussed, perhaps for the next major F# version, is a subset of features that make usage of FharpPlus more pleasant for folks who rely on the library. But some of that might not involve typeclasses at all. I think it's a worthy exercise to figure out though.

@ShalokShalom I think that's incorrect; core Scala development is very much centralized in EPFL (Scala 3) and Lightbend (Scala 2.x); so much so that a subset of the Scala community feels it's not independent _enough_ 🙂. At least that's the impression I get.

@Happypig375 SRTPs are not even close.
SRTPs as of now (v4.6) only work if you define members at the original type declaration which is a massive limitation in usage. 
In that case you might be better of to simply implement interfaces on your type declaration

